"""Original communicable-disease drill; the first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
A pathogen is|an infectious agent that can cause disease|always a mosquito|a guarantee of symptoms in every host|a method of measuring height|A pathogen is an organism or infectious agent capable of causing disease. A pathogen and the symptoms it produces are not the same thing.
Infection can occur|without noticeable symptoms in some people|only when every symptom is visible|only after a bone injury|only during a sports event|Some infections do not produce noticeable symptoms. Absence of visible symptoms does not establish that infection is absent.
A communicable disease can|be transmitted between hosts under suitable conditions|never spread by any route|only result from a fracture|be diagnosed from clothing|Communicable diseases can spread directly or indirectly under suitable conditions. Spread depends on the infectious agent, route and circumstances.
Which group includes infectious agents?|viruses, bacteria, fungi and parasites|only bones and joints|only vitamins and minerals|only muscles and tendons|Several classes of infectious agents can cause disease. Not every organism in these groups causes disease.
A transmission route is|a pathway by which an agent can reach a host|a guarantee that every contact causes illness|a complete treatment plan|a personal name|A route describes how an infectious agent can reach a host. A route identifies a possible pathway, not a certain outcome.
Different infections|can have different transmission routes|always spread in exactly the same way|never involve the environment|are diagnosed by the same symptom in every case|Transmission depends on the agent and circumstances.
Respiratory infections can spread through|infectious particles in the air|only a relay baton by definition|only a bone diagram|only a genetic trait|Some respiratory infections spread through infectious particles in air.
Some diarrhoeal infections can spread through|contaminated food or water|only mosquito bites|a person's name|a joint classification|Contaminated food and water can transmit some diarrhoeal pathogens.
A vector can|transmit an infectious agent between hosts|always be the pathogen itself|make all water safe|provide a diagnosis without testing|A vector is an organism that can transmit an agent.
A preventive measure is most useful when|matched to the relevant transmission route|assumed identical for every disease|selected without considering access|claimed to eliminate all risk|Prevention should address the actual pathogen and pathway.
Malaria is caused by|Plasmodium parasites|all mosquitoes themselves|a lack of dietary fibre|a bone injury|Plasmodium parasites cause malaria.
Malaria is commonly transmitted through|bites of infected female Anopheles mosquitoes|ordinary hugging|sharing food in routine social contact|all insect bites|Infected female Anopheles mosquitoes are common malaria vectors.
A fever in a malaria-risk area|has several possible causes and needs appropriate assessment|always proves malaria|never requires attention|is always caused by a virus|Fever is not specific to malaria and needs appropriate evaluation.
A suspected malaria case should generally be|assessed and tested according to local clinical guidance|diagnosed only from the time of day|treated with another person's medicine without assessment|ignored if the person has had malaria before|Appropriate assessment and testing help guide diagnosis and treatment.
Which symptom with suspected malaria requires urgent assessment?|confusion or difficulty breathing|a preference for a particular food|a change in shirt colour|a completed school assignment|Confusion or breathing difficulty can signal severe illness and needs urgent help.
Insecticide-treated nets can|reduce mosquito bites and malaria risk when used appropriately|guarantee no person ever becomes ill|make contaminated water safe|replace all clinical assessment|Treated nets are an important part of malaria prevention but do not eliminate all risk.
A malaria vaccine or preventive medicine|may be available for particular groups under local guidance|makes every other measure unnecessary|is automatically appropriate for every person|treats every cause of fever|Eligibility and suitability depend on current programmes and individual circumstances.
Safe water and sanitation can help reduce|some food- and waterborne infections|every disease by a single mechanism|only sports injuries|the need for all medical care|Safe water and sanitation interrupt important pathways for some infections.
Diarrhoea can cause|dehydration|a guaranteed increase in hydration|a bone turning into a tendon|immunity to all infections|Fluid loss from diarrhoea can cause dehydration.
Severe diarrhoea or inability to drink|can require prompt clinical care|should always be ignored|is diagnosed from a textbook alone|is always caused by one pathogen|Severe symptoms and inability to drink require timely assessment.
Tuberculosis is caused by|bacteria|a dietary mineral alone|a mosquito as the pathogen|a fracture|TB is caused by bacteria, commonly affecting the lungs.
Infectious pulmonary tuberculosis can spread|through the air|only by mosquito bite|only through contaminated water|through ordinary genetic inheritance|Infectious pulmonary TB can spread through airborne particles.
A persistent cough|warrants appropriate assessment and does not prove TB by itself|always proves TB|never needs assessment|is a complete laboratory diagnosis|A persistent cough has several possible causes and needs assessment.
Ventilation can help reduce exposure to|some airborne infectious particles|all chemical contamination of water|every mosquito bite|every possible health risk|Ventilation can reduce exposure to some airborne pathogens in suitable settings.
HIV is|a virus that affects the immune system|a bacterium causing malaria|a bone disease|a mosquito species|HIV is a virus that affects the immune system.
HIV can be transmitted through|certain body-fluid exposures such as contaminated blood|ordinary hugging|sharing food in routine social contact|a handshake alone|Certain blood and other body-fluid exposures can transmit HIV; casual contact does not.
HIV is not transmitted through|ordinary hugging and sharing food|some contaminated blood exposures|certain sexual exposures|some parent-to-baby transmission|WHO states that HIV is not spread by ordinary social contact such as hugging or sharing food.
Sharing contaminated needles can|transmit HIV and other blood-borne infections|guarantee protection from infection|be treated as ordinary social contact without blood exposure|replace testing|Contaminated needles can transmit infections through blood exposure.
HIV can pass from a parent to a baby|during pregnancy, birth or breastfeeding|only through a handshake|only through mosquito bites|only through sharing a desk|Parent-to-baby transmission can occur during pregnancy, birth or breastfeeding; care can reduce risk.
Antiretroviral therapy for HIV|treats infection and can suppress the virus but is not a cure|is a guaranteed cure|is an antibiotic for all bacterial infections|makes individual care unnecessary|ART treats HIV and can suppress viral load, but does not cure HIV.
A person with a sustained undetectable HIV viral load|does not sexually transmit HIV according to WHO guidance|can transmit HIV through hugging|needs no ongoing care|is automatically cured|WHO states that people with sustained undetectable viral load do not sexually transmit HIV.
Correct and consistent condom use|reduces the risk of HIV and many other STIs|guarantees no possible risk in every situation|replaces all testing and advice|transmits HIV through intact material|Condoms reduce risk when used correctly and consistently, but do not remove every risk.
Stigma towards a person with an infection|can create barriers to care and support|is required for prevention|is a medical treatment|establishes the route of infection|Stigma can discourage testing, support and treatment.
Vaccination|trains the immune system to recognise particular infectious agents|protects equally against every disease|replaces all other preventive measures|is a complete diagnosis|Vaccines target particular agents and are one part of prevention.
A vaccine schedule|should follow current local guidance and individual advice|is identical for every vaccine and person|never needs updating|replaces assessment of symptoms|Schedules vary by vaccine, programme and circumstances.
Antibiotics|treat susceptible bacterial infections but not viruses|treat every viral infection|replace all vaccines|prove that every fever is bacterial|Antibiotics work against susceptible bacteria, not viral infections.
Testing for infection can|help identify a cause and guide treatment|guarantee that every symptom has the same cause|replace all clinical judgement|always be interpreted without context|Testing is one part of diagnosis and care.
A mosquito net does not|make drinking water safe|help reduce mosquito bites when used correctly|form part of malaria prevention|require appropriate use|A net targets mosquito exposure, not contamination in drinking water.
Sharing accurate information about HIV should include|respect for privacy and avoidance of discrimination|claiming ordinary hugging transmits HIV|assuming a person's status from appearance|withholding prevention information|Respectful, evidence-based communication avoids stigma and misinformation.
Infection prevention is strengthened by|matching actions to routes and supporting access to care|claiming one action prevents every infection|assuming symptoms always prove a diagnosis|ignoring environmental conditions|Different routes require appropriate interventions, and access to care matters.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
